        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe feature is wired coherently overall (schema across all four dialects, entity field + accessors, service method, cap applied at the single derivation point in the mapper), but a few structural issues stand out:\n\n1. **Dead local variable in `addOwner` (OwnerRestControllerV1).** The diff removes the only consumer of `householdExists` (the `if (householdExists && !sharesHousehold)` CONFLICT block) but leaves the declaration:\n   ```java\n   boolean householdExists = this.clinicService.existsOwnerInHousehold(owner.getHouseholdId());\n   ```\n   Nothing in the diff reads it anymore. Either remove it, or if it's still needed, that's not visible here. As written it reads as leftover code a maintainer would trip over. (Separately worth confirming: dropping the household-duplicate CONFLICT rejection entirely looks like collateral removal unrelated to the cap spec \u2014 that block and the new cap logic are independent concerns and the spec only asked for the cap.)\n\n2. **Entity mutation to drive a derived read (`computeMembershipLevelCap`).** The helper does `member.setHouseholdSize(members.size())` on each managed entity purely so `formatMembershipLevel` can compute a value. Priming a persistent field on loaded entities as a side effect of a read-only ceiling calculation is a leaky coupling \u2014 the caller has to know the mapper's internal dependency on `householdSize`, and it mutates rows fetched in a read-only transaction. If `formatMembershipLevel` needs a household size, that dependency should be passed explicitly rather than smuggled through entity state.\n\n3. **Placement of `computeMembershipLevelCap`.** Deriving \"one above the max membership level among household members\" is business logic that leans on the service (`findOwnersByHouseholdId`) *and* the presentation mapper (`formatMembershipLevel`) and iterates the collection \u2014 it sits more naturally in the service layer alongside `findOwnersByHouseholdId` (or as a dedicated method) than in the controller. Reusing the mapper's threshold logic to avoid duplicating the points\u2192level bands is good; doing it from the controller while mutating entities is the awkward part. Consider moving the whole computation behind the service so the controller just calls `clinicService.computeMembershipLevelCap(householdId)`.\n\nPoints 2 and 3 are related: pushing the computation into the service and passing size explicitly would remove the entity mutation at the same time."
